::CW_U06_L08_GP_Q01::[MLA.CW.WR.08] A question asks which portfolio piece best shows growth. Which answer is strongest?{
~A. The piece the writer likes most but cannot explain.#Incorrect. Preference alone does not show growth. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=B. The piece revised from feedback with a clear craft improvement.#Correct. This provides evidence of growth and author control.
~C. The piece with no revision history.#Incorrect. Growth is easier to show when revision evidence is present.
~D. The piece with the most pages only.#Incorrect. Length alone does not prove growth. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U06_L08_GP_Q02::[MLA.CW.COM.03] A final file has a missing title and visible draft comments. What skill is being tested?{
=A. Publication readiness.#Correct. Title, draft comments, and final file presentation are publication-readiness issues.
~B. Character motivation only.#Incorrect. The issue is final presentation, not character development.
~C. Meter only.#Incorrect. The question is not focused on poetry rhythm.
~D. Research citation only.#Incorrect. This unit focuses on portfolio and publication decisions.
}

::CW_U06_L08_GP_Q03::[MLA.CW.REV.01] Which answer best shows craft reasoning?{
~A. The story is about moving away.#Incorrect. This is topic summary. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~B. I worked hard.#Incorrect. Effort is not craft reasoning. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=C. I used clipped dialogue so the reader feels the tension between speakers.#Correct. This names a craft choice and effect.
~D. It is my favorite scene.#Incorrect. Preference does not explain craft. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U06_L08_GP_Q04::[MLA.CW.PUB.02] Which presentation plan is strongest?{
~A. Read the excerpt with no title or focus.#Incorrect. The audience needs orientation. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~B. Summarize the whole portfolio without reading anything.#Incorrect. The task requires focused presentation of creative work.
~C. Apologize for the draft and stop.#Incorrect. This does not present author craft. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=D. Introduce title and genre, give brief context, read a focused excerpt, and explain effect.#Correct. This follows the author presentation process. This answer matches the lesson skill and is supported by the specific creative-writing evidence in the prompt.
}

::CW_U06_L08_GP_Q05::[MLA.CW.COM.01] Which answer should you avoid on a synthesis assessment?{
~A. The answer with specific evidence and reader effect.#Incorrect. That is usually a strong answer. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=B. The vague answer that says "make it better" without a specific action.#Correct. Vague answers do not show mastery. This answer matches the lesson skill and is supported by the specific creative-writing evidence in the prompt.
~C. The answer that connects revision to craft growth.#Incorrect. That supports synthesis. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~D. The answer that identifies publication formatting problems.#Incorrect. That is useful when the question is about readiness.
}
